// Attribution of a single aid (L. 821-5, R. 821-1/3/4/5/6, L. 841-5):
// eligibility joined with the request, the literal APL priority, the ALS
// same-purpose difference, dwelling unicity markers and the
// allocation-to-APL switch.
//
// READING ADOPTED (flagged for the owner): R. 821-5 is read LITERALLY — when
// APL scope conditions are met, only APL is attributed, even if the household
// requested ALF or ALS (the household is redirected to an APL request; the
// ALF/ALS attribution is defeated on bare eligible_apl, with no APL-demand
// premise). The coherent alternative (priority among requested aids only)
// would pay ALF to an APL-eligible household; the text says "seule cette
// aide est attribuee" without conditioning on the demand.
//
// The L. 821-5 non-cumul emerges from the priority defeaters; no separate
// rule restates it. R. 821-1 (one person/household, several dwellings) is
// its own prohibition marker. The one-aid-per-dwelling default that
// R. 821-4 derogates from is a conflict marker with the R. 821-4
// co-ownership/co-tenancy defeater. R. 821-3 (separated spouses, two
// established principal residences) is a permission marker: each spouse may
// be granted an aid; distinct subjects in distinct dwellings escape the
// same-household/single-dwelling logic by construction, so no defeater is
// needed. Which attribution is withdrawn on a proven conflict stays a
// boundary (unpinned enforcement).
//
// FRAGMENTS (predicted; reviewer verifies after sync --write): base + all 7
// _ED fragments per cited article (all stable since 2019-09-01).

language "law.core" version "0.2";
package fr.apl version "0.1.0";
namespace "urn:fr:corpus:clir:apl";

